"newValue": "Vitéz Ferenc Feketehalmy-Czeydner, a Hungarian military officer, rose through the ranks of the Royal Hungarian Army to attain the position of Lieutenant General, becoming a figure inextricably linked to the brutal wartime atrocities committed by the Hungarian forces. His career was shaped by the interwar period's rising nationalism and Hungary's alliance with the Axis powers, culminating in his command during the notorious Novi Sad massacre. This event, a systematic extermination campaign against Serbs, Jews, and Romani people in January 1942, cemented his place in the annals of war criminality.

Feketehalmy-Czeydner, then commander of the Hungarian V Corps, was directly responsible for ordering and overseeing the massacre in Novi Sad and other settlements in southern Bačka. Over three days, between January 21 and 23, 1942, Hungarian forces under his command executed between 3,000 and 4,000 civilians, including women, children, and the elderly. The victims were rounded up, taken to the frozen Danube or Tisza rivers, and shot before their bodies were thrown into the icy waters. This act of collective punishment, officially presented as an anti-partisan operation, was a pre-meditated ethnic cleansing operation carried out with extreme cruelty.

After the war, Feketehalmy-Czeydner initially fled to Germany but was eventually apprehended. He was extradited to Yugoslavia, where he faced trial alongside other Hungarian officers for their roles in the Novi Sad massacre. Convicted of war crimes and crimes against humanity, he was sentenced to death. His execution by firing squad in Žabalj, Yugoslavia, on November 5, 1946, served as a powerful, albeit belated, act of justice for the thousands of innocent lives he had ordered to be extinguished. His actions remain a dark chapter in Hungarian military history and a stark reminder of the horrors of ethnic violence during World War II."}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-30T23:25:25Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#41142582987", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Modern era: industrialization, imperial expansion, and democratic revolutions", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1890-11-22, Simeria"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": "1890-11-22, Simeria"}], "newValue": ["The rise of extreme nationalism and irredentist sentiments in interwar Hungary fostered an environment conducive to ethnic violence.", "Hungary's strategic alliance with Nazi Germany and the Axis powers during World War II provided military and ideological cover for atrocities.", "The perceived need to suppress real or imagined partisan activities in occupied territories led to brutal reprisal policies against civilian populations."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-30T23:25:25Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#41142582987", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "1946-11-05"}, {"title": "Contributed to mass society, world wars, and foundations of contemporary global order", "type": "Movement", "year": "1946-11-05"}], "newValue": ["The Novi Sad massacre resulted in the systematic extermination of thousands of Serbian, Jewish, and Romani